      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The statement assumes only an 'effectively encoded' X. The opened encoding definition calls an encoding effective when it has a specified total decoder, but expressly remarks that this 'does not yet formalize computation by Turing machines.' Hence the decoder need not be Turing-computable. Let e_X:N->E be a bijection onto an infinite non-c.e. binary set E, with its set-theoretic total decoder, and let a machine halt on every input while outputting a fixed singleton code. It computes a total function X->{*}, whose domain is X; if X were c.e. under e_X, its computable enumerator would enumerate E, a contradiction. The proof's instruction to 'use the total decoder' as a machine procedure is likewise unlicensed.",
