      "evidence": "Recognizability is defined for raw words over Sigma, whereas computable enumerability is defined relative to a chosen merely 'effective' word encoding. That encoding need only have a specified set-theoretic decoder; the dependency explicitly says this does not formalize Turing computation. Choose an encoding of Sigma* whose image is an infinite non-c.e. binary set E. Then L=Sigma* has the trivial always-accepting recognizer, but a total computable enumerator for L under that encoding would enumerate E. Thus the equivalence needs a Turing-computable coding/decoding hypothesis and is false as stated.",
